  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1141 AM MDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nDeep upper trough over the PAC NW and western Canada will support\ngeneral SW flow into the weekend. Departing impulse giving way to\nclear skies today as winds shift easterly behind the sfc \nreflection. Expect highs in the 50s and 60s. Southerly flow will \ndevelop tonight and support a push of low level moisture into the \nregion. This will support low clouds and areas of fog over most of\nthe SD Plains. Temps will struggle to rise Friday on the SD \nPlains with 40s and 50s expected for highs. With clear skies and \nbetter mixing, expect 60s over northeast WY. There is a low chance\nfor a few showers Friday night toward central SD as a disturbance\nskirts the region to the south and forces pos theta-e adv in the \nNorthern Plains. Low clouds and fog are likely to stick around on \nthe SD Plains as southerly flow persists Friday night. Winds will \nveer in the low levels Sat and allow mix out of low clouds and fog\nas drier are mix in. However, mid and upper level clouds look to \nincrease which could hold temps down a bit. Western CONUS upper \ntrough will lift out of the western Pac early next week, with \nbroad lift spreading into the region starting Sunday night. Ample \nlow level moisture will advect ahead of this system which will \nallow for a decent chance of precip on the SD Plains, esp toward \ncentral SD. The best chance for precip will be Monday when the \nmain trough passes over the region. Very warm system for early \nNov, with mainly rain expected. However, enough cold air may \nentrain into the back of the system Monday to allow for some \nhigher elevation snow across northeast WY and the Black Hills. \nThis could some support some light snow accums there, esp in the \nnorthern Black Hills. Things will dry out west to east Tues with \ncooler conds expected for the rest of the week as NW flow becomes \nestablished.",
